WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.572
Testing safer string usage means testing
the contract, not just the happy path.

2
00:00:04.652 --> 00:00:08.176
Boundary tests should cover empty strings,
one-character strings,

3
00:00:08.256 --> 00:00:11.295
full buffers, maximum accepted lengths,
exact-fit cases,

4
00:00:11.375 --> 00:00:13.016
and one-byte-too-large cases.

5
00:00:13.096 --> 00:00:16.803
These tests show whether capacity and
terminator handling are correct.

6
00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:19.192
Truncation tests are especially important.

7
00:00:19.272 --> 00:00:23.526
If an API reports truncation, the program
should respond according to the use case.

8
00:00:23.606 --> 00:00:25.186
Display text may be shortened.

9
00:00:25.266 --> 00:00:27.271
A configuration value may be rejected.

10
00:00:27.351 --> 00:00:29.357
A protocol field may need a clear error.

11
00:00:29.437 --> 00:00:33.630
The test should confirm the intended
decision, not merely that the call returned.

12
00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:37.903
Dynamic string libraries need
allocation-failure and maximum-size tests where

13
00:00:37.983 --> 00:00:38.591
practical.

14
00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:43.168
Builders, append operations, and
allocation-backed formatting should leave objects

15
00:00:43.248 --> 00:00:45.132
in a defined state when growth fails.

16
00:00:45.212 --> 00:00:48.007
Cleanup should work on both success and
failure paths.

17
00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:52.341
Embedded-null tests are useful when the
code crosses between byte buffers and text

18
00:00:52.421 --> 00:00:52.907
strings.

19
00:00:52.987 --> 00:00:54.385
If binary data is possible,

20
00:00:54.465 --> 00:00:58.232
tests should confirm that the selected API
does not stop early or silently

21
00:00:58.312 --> 00:01:02.506
reinterpret the data as a shorter string
unless that is exactly the intended rule.

22
00:01:02.586 --> 00:01:03.619
Tool support helps.

23
00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:07.041
Compiler warnings can find suspicious
sizes or ignored results.

24
00:01:07.121 --> 00:01:10.767
Static analysis can flag unsafe call
patterns and ownership mistakes.

25
00:01:10.847 --> 00:01:13.217
Sanitizers can reveal runtime memory
errors.

26
00:01:13.297 --> 00:01:16.214
Fortified diagnostics may add checks on
some platforms.

27
00:01:16.294 --> 00:01:20.305
These tools support review, but they do
not replace clear contracts and tests.

28
00:01:20.385 --> 00:01:23.120
Code review should ask the same questions
every time:

29
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.150
what representation is this, what capacity
is accurate, who owns the buffer,

30
00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:31.241
what happens on truncation or allocation
failure, and was the result checked?

31
00:01:31.321 --> 00:01:34.420
Consistent review turns safer library use
into a team habit.
